{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Intervention executed: `docker builder prune -f` on the production host. **Root 72% -> 65%, 4.93 GB reclaimed.** Services verified healthy afterwards.\n\n**Why I did this rather than continuing to defer.** The trough measurement above changed the reasoning. While the floor was flat at 56-59 the sawtooth genuinely was bounded and waiting cost nothing; once troughs began climbing (63 -> 68 -> 69), \"it self-reclaims, so it can wait\" stopped being accurate, and at ~3 points/hour on the floor the 85% alarm was about five hours out on a path that does not retreat.\n\n**Pre-state, measured immediately before (not inferred):**\n\n    /dev/nvme0n1p1  62837740  45189568  17648172  72% /\n    Build Cache   107 entries   0 ACTIVE   5.877GB   5.877GB RECLAIMABLE\n\nZero active entries is the fact that made this safe: no build was using the cache, so nothing in flight could be pulled out from under. 100% reclaimable meant no judgement call about what to keep.\n\n**Post-state:**\n\n    /dev/nvme0n1p1  62837740  40263832  22573908  65% /\n    Build Cache   25 entries   0 ACTIVE   0B   0B\n\nReclaimed 4,925,736 KB = **4.93 GB**, seven percentage points, and free space went 17.6 GB -> 22.6 GB. The new floor (65%) is below every trough recorded since 07:51 UTC.\n\n**Health verified after, not assumed.** All four containers still running — `compose-review-bot-1 (healthy)`, `rebar-mcp-59d504c2bdbb-8093 (healthy)`, `compose-opcert-1`, `compose-gerrit-1`. Gerrit returns HTTP 200 on `localhost:8080` and 200 over HTTPS from outside. Images, containers and volumes were untouched: no `system prune`, no `-f` on containers, no volume removal.\n\n**Cost, stated up front:** the next autodeploy build runs COLD, so it will be minutes slower and root will rise further than usual during it. That is the price and it was worth paying against a rising floor.\n\n**What this did NOT fix, and I want that on the record.** This bought headroom; it did not fix a cap. The cache was 5.877 GB against a `builder.gc.maxUsedSpace` of 5.00 GiB — roughly 9% OVER its own budget — and pruning it to zero does not establish that the budget will hold next time. Whether `builder.gc` is misconfigured, not running, or behaving as the best-effort target it is documented to be is still open, and it is epic 6202's AC3 (\"each named root accumulator has an ENFORCED cap\"). If the cache simply regrows past 5.00 GiB, that is the answer, and it is worth watching for over the next few hours.\n\nRelated: the overshoot was invisible in `docker_buildkit_cache_used_percent` because that metric is clamped at 100 — bug `undefiled-revivable-gemsbok`. Until that lands, the cache crossing its budget again will render as a healthy-looking pinned ceiling, so the regrowth check has to be made against `docker system df` directly, not the metric.\n\nThe other measured reclaims remain untaken and still need an operator: ~3.75 GB of nine-day-old `/var/tmp/rebar-review-rehearsal.*` directories, and 958 MB of reclaimable image layers. I took only the one that was 100% reclaimable with zero active entries."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788605843082168923,"uuid":"a00bb00f-d431-4e2e-84d5-fa0fb98fcd08"}